**Constructor and destructor.** A constructor runs when an object is created and sets it to a valid state. A destructor runs automatically when the object's lifetime ends and releases resources.

    class Buffer {
        int* data;
        size_t n;
    public:
        Buffer(size_t size) : data(new int[size]), n(size) {}
        ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }
        Buffer(const Buffer&) = delete;            // avoid double free
        Buffer& operator=(const Buffer&) = delete;
    };

**Why destructors are needed:** to free resources the object owns (heap memory, file handles, sockets, locks). Because the destructor is called deterministically when the object goes out of scope, even during exceptions (stack unwinding), C++ uses **RAII**: tie a resource's lifetime to an object. Prefer `std::vector`, `std::unique_ptr` and `std::lock_guard` so you rarely write a manual destructor. Base classes used polymorphically need a **virtual destructor**, otherwise deleting a derived object through a base pointer is undefined behaviour. Follow the rule of three/five when you manage a resource.

**Memory leak.** Memory allocated on the heap that is never freed and can no longer be reached, so the program's memory use grows over time (for example `new` without `delete`, an early return or exception between them, or a cyclic `shared_ptr`). Effects are slowdowns and eventual out-of-memory. Detect with Valgrind or AddressSanitizer, and prevent with RAII and smart pointers.

**Functor (function object).** A class or struct that overloads `operator()`, so an object can be called like a function. Unlike a plain function pointer it can **hold state** and the compiler can inline the call.

    struct AddN {
        int n;
        int operator()(int x) const { return x + n; }
    };
    // AddN add5{5}; add5(10) == 15; also usable in std::transform or std::sort

Lambdas are essentially compiler-generated functors.

**Dynamic linking.** Library code is not copied into the executable; it is loaded and bound at load time or run time (`.so` on Linux, `.dll` on Windows). Advantages: smaller executables, shared memory across processes, and updating a library without rebuilding. Costs: dependency and version problems, and slightly slower startup. Static linking copies the library into the binary, which is self-contained but larger.
